{"posts":[{"id":"quasimodo_p19_451","t":"kruger wrote:\n\nAndyfx wrote: I read several times the Marketpedia but still the QM dynamic is not very clear to me. While a QM is forming, the engulf of previous demand for us is a strong signal that price wants to go lower, so we wait the FTR to sell at Supply of Higher High (Head) or previous High (left Shoulder). My question is: why the big market players let engulfing previous demand, wich tells us that price wants to reverse? If they need to fill their sell order, isn't enough waiting for people buyng at a pullback of the trend (higher low in an uptrend) and suddenly push the price lower without any warning? What kind of benefit do they have from engulfing previous demand and not simply consume it to prepare the sell off? Probably I don't understand the market dynamic behind the QM and why it occours the way it does, but I can't figure out why. What do you think?\n\nYep I read this on If journal recently, but also I was wondering: reatail traders volume in the forex market is like only 10% or so (the 10 major banks have something like 75% of total forex volume) so aren't big players in competition between each other?","a":"Andyfx","d":"","n":"#451","p":19,"im":[],"m":451},{"id":"quasimodo_p19_452","t":"QM on AJ and AU","a":"Sharky","d":"","n":"#452","p":19,"im":["b3e451fe44a0172958e1103a6eee9177a7855dc3","8f6c547761860691defd90fc88746efa1fb711ea"],"m":452},{"id":"quasimodo_p19_453","t":"Here is some more HW i have been working on.\n\nComments and critique PLEASE, love to hear your opinions, thanks","a":"sassofrass","d":"","n":"#453","p":19,"im":["508f4b1dc4a368f448bcae3e846a74b010d9eed4","fd772e79b3357007ecb58d281085aef85f723a1b","99fad8ead404259f6b71e1cf581728510bcd50c0","7ba8a7a83365c82652aab14838b97c7acf4e9710","a2b4032ca09ffc42ccdad0b83ce3f7279f8f36ea","d06ad972f5a038ce3b435e9b413c9df8d4789854"],"m":453},{"id":"quasimodo_p19_454","t":"Good Job Sharky and sassofrass\n\n@Sharky I noticed that QM formation on Aussie too...let's see what happens. Weekly Demand almost touched, zooming into Daily there is some more space.\n\n@sassofrass Be careful with QM formations in the mid zone (first chart), QM at the extremes into fresh SD zones are more reliable","a":"Andyfx","d":"","n":"#454","p":19,"im":[],"m":454},{"id":"quasimodo_p19_455","t":"Sharky i also caught that AUD/USD QM and there is one on the Daily CHF too.","a":"sassofrass","d":"","n":"#455","p":19,"im":["631e716471a59410d1ca820f673de468baff6cb0"],"m":455},{"id":"quasimodo_p19_456","t":"Hotochin wrote:\n\nkruger wrote:\n\nAndyfx wrote: I read several times the Marketpedia but still the QM dynamic is not very clear to me. While a QM is forming, the engulf of previous demand for us is a strong signal that price wants to go lower, so we wait the FTR to sell at Supply of Higher High (Head) or previous High (left Shoulder). My question is: why the big market players let engulfing previous demand, wich tells us that price wants to reverse? If they need to fill their sell order, isn't enough waiting for people buyng at a pullback of the trend (higher low in an uptrend) and suddenly push the price lower without any warning? What kind of benefit do they have from engulfing previous\n\ndemand and not simply consume it to prepare the sell off? Probably I don't understand the market dynamic behind the QM and why it occours the way it does, but I can't figure out why. What do you think?\n\nI agree that QM is important but I wouldn't go that far. For example we can have nice QM on M1 but main move from HTF is against our QM so there is only a small chance for reaction if any.","a":"kruger","d":"","n":"#456","p":19,"im":[],"m":456},{"id":"quasimodo_p19_457","t":"Andyfx wrote:\n\nkruger wrote:\n\nAndyfx wrote: I read several times the Marketpedia but still the QM dynamic is not very clear to me. While a QM is forming, the engulf of previous demand for us is a strong signal that price wants to go lower, so we wait the FTR to sell at Supply of